.TH SYSREPORT-SEARCH 1 "October 2026" "srs-sysinfo 2.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sysreport-search \- search a native system report, render report items and look up PCI/USB IDs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysreport-search
[\fB\-h\fR] [\fB\-\-version\fR] {\fBid\fR,\fBfind\fR,\fBdevices\fR,\fB\-save\fR,\fB\-\-list\fR,\fBguide\fR,\fBburn\fR,\fBbench\fR,\fBipi\fR,\fBlatency\fR,\fBpi\fR,\fBcrypto\fR,\fBprime\fR,\fBcachemem\fR,\fBstorage\fR,\fBos\-latency\fR,\fBcompute\fR,\fBwork\fR,\fBnet\fR,\fBgpu\fR,\fBgpu\-burn\fR,\fBgpu\-compare\fR,\fBcuda\-toolkit\fR} ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
SIV-style search for the native system report written by
.BR srs-sysreport (1),
device-ID lookups against the hwdata databases, the report items of
.BR srs-gui (1)
as text, and the CPU benchmarks and stress test.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBid\fR \fIVVVV\fR[:\fIDDDD\fR[:\fISSSS\fR:\fIssss\fR]][:\fIRR\fR] [\fB\-\-bus\fR pci|usb] [\fB\-\-mode\fR tree|scan|info]
hierarchical device-ID lookup, most specific key first.
.TP
\fBfind\fR \fIPATTERN\fR [\fB\-\-report\fR \fIDIR\fR] [\fB\-i\fR] [\fB\-F\fR] [\fB\-\-max\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR auto|always|never]
regular-expression search across a report's sections with highlighted matches.
.TP
\fBdevices\fR [\fB\-\-bus\fR pci|usb|all]
resolve every local PCI/USB device.
.TP
\fB\-save\fR[=[\fIitem\fR][\fIitem\fR:\fIarg\fR]...][=\fIFILE\fR|STDOUT] [\fB\-\-redact\fR]
render report items as text (no items = the default set, [all] = every item).
\fB\-\-redact\fR masks serial numbers, UUIDs, MAC addresses (vendor half kept),
boot/machine IDs and the host name, for reports you share.
Quote the argument in zsh or fish ([..] is a glob there).
.TP
\fB\-\-list\fR
list every item: id, category, bucket, group, title.
.TP
\fBguide\fR [\fIITEM\fR] [\fB\-\-print\fR]
open the user guide at \fIITEM\fR's section (PDF viewer, else the HTML guide);
\fB\-\-print\fR only shows the section and the command that would run.
.TP
\fBburn\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR | \fB\-\-cpus\fR \fILIST\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-mode\fR same|swap] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIC\fR] [\fB\-\-cool\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-cycles\fR \fIN\fR]
CPU burn-in with a temperature limit (default 10 s, limit 90 C).
The summary of each run is saved to \fI~/.cache/srs/burnin\-YYYYMMDD\-HHMMSS.txt\fR.
.TP
\fBbench\fR {ac,as,dd,dw,ec,ep,lc,lp,pc,pp} [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Dhrystone / Whetstone in a SIV benchmark mode (0.1..60 s per measurement); asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBipi\fR [\fB\-\-cpus\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-rounds\fR \fIR\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
core-to-core (cache-line ping-pong) latency matrix (two CPUs busy at a time); asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR. With one CPU there is no pair: it exits with 2 and keeps the last result.
.TP
\fBlatency\fR [\fB\-\-max\fR \fIMB\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
memory latency by block size, up to \fIMB\fR (1..4096, default 256; one CPU); asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBpi\fR [\fB\-\-digits\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-method\fR \fBchudnovsky\fR|\fBagm\fR] [\fB\-\-save\-digits\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
SRS Pi: pi to \fIN\fR decimal places (128k..32m, or SuperPi sizes 16ki..32mi; default 1m, 1 thread); every digit is checked. Asks first, with the time, the threads and the memory it needs (up to about 1.6 GB for 32mi), unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBcrypto\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
AES\-128 and SHA\-256 speed in MB/s, with AES\-NI / SHA\-NI and in plain C; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBprime\fR [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fB1e8\fR|\fB1e9\fR|\fB1e10\fR|\fB1e11\fR|\fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
count the primes up to the limit (any number from 100 to 1e11, e.g. 5e9 or 1g; default 1e9, all threads), checked against the known count; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBcachemem\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-isa\fR \fBavx512\fR|\fBavx2\fR|\fBsse2\fR] [\fB\-\-mem\-mib\fR \fIM\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIlevels\fR] [\fB\-\-tests\fR \fItests\fR] [\fB\-\-mib\fR] [\fB\-\-mem\-write\fR \fImode\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Cache & Memory benchmark: Read / Write / Copy and Latency of the RAM and L1\-L3 (all threads, about 20 s; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR).
.TP
\fBstorage\fR \fB\-\-dir\fR \fIFOLDER\fR [\fB\-\-size\fR \fIMB\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-profile\fR \fBstandard\fR|\fBlight\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-usb\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
read / write speed of the drive holding \fIFOLDER\fR, on a temporary test file that is unlinked at once; existing files are never touched; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBos\-latency\fR [\fB\-\-samples\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-tick\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIgroups\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
how quickly the OS answers: system calls, context switches, thread / process start, wake\-ups, timer lateness (with the CPU scheduler in use), page faults and small\-file create / stat / unlink, as median / 95% / 99% / worst (one or two CPUs, about 6 s; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR). \fIgroups\fR: syscall, switch, spawn, wake, timer, memory, files.
.TP
\fBcompute\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-tests\fR \fItests\fR] [\fB\-\-code\fR \fBauto\fR|\fBc\fR|\fBsse2\fR|\fBavx2\fR|\fBavx512\fR] [\fB\-\-single\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
CPU Compute: matrix multiply, peak multiply\-add, FFT, N\-body, Mandelbrot and a ray tracer in GFLOPS / Mpixels/s on 1 and N threads, every answer checked (WRONG instead of a score). All threads are 100% busy (AVX\-512 where available; an unstable overclock or undervolt may crash the PC); \fB\-\-single\fR keeps it light; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBwork\fR [\fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIgroups\fR] [\fB\-\-libs\fR \fIlibs\fR] [\fB\-\-single\fR] [\fB\-\-part\-kib\fR \fIK\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Everyday work: compression with the system's zlib, zstd, lz4 and xz (loaded at run time; a missing one is skipped), sorting, checksums, text processing and a bytecode interpreter on 1 and N threads, every result checked; generated data only; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR. \fIgroups\fR: compress, sort, hash, text, interp.
.TP
\fBnet\fR [\fB\-\-local\fR] [\fB\-\-internet\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-streams\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-samples\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-mb\fR \fIMB\fR] [\fB\-4\fR|\fB\-6\fR] [\fB\-\-server\fR \fIURL\fR] [\fB\-\-hosts\fR \fIHOST\fR[:\fIPORT\fR],...] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\-internet\fR]
Network benchmark. Without options: loopback TCP / UDP throughput, loss and round trip time (nothing leaves the computer; asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR). \fB\-\-internet\fR adds DNS, connect, HTTPS, download and upload tests against https://speed.cloudflare.com; it prints what it contacts and the data use (up to about 4 x \fB\-\-max\-mb\fR, default 1000 MB) and asks its own question, which only \fB\-\-yes\-internet\fR skips. libcurl is optional (loaded at run time). No addresses or host names are shown or saved.
.TP
\fBgpu\fR [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-only\fR \fItests\fR] [\fB\-\-mem\-mib\fR \fIM\fR] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
GPU benchmark through Vulkan: FP32, FP16, FP64 and INT32 multiply\-add (GFLOPS / GIOPS), video memory read, write and copy, upload and download (GB/s) and dispatch latency (µs), every result checked. The Vulkan loader is opened at run time (optional). \fB\-\-list\fR lists the Vulkan devices and runs nothing. Asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR; exit code 2 when it did not start. \fItests\fR: fp32, fp16, fp64, int32, read, write, copy, upload, download, latency.
.TP
\fBgpu\-burn\fR [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-backend\fR \fBvulkan\fR|\fBhip\fR|\fBcuda\fR|\fBopencl\fR|\fBauto\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR|\fBall\fR] [\fB\-\-fp32\fR|\fB\-\-fp64\fR] [\fB\-\-limit\fR \fIC\fR] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
GPU Burnin: repeated matrix multiplications at full GPU load through Vulkan, ROCm/HIP, CUDA or OpenCL (all loaded at run time), every result compared with the first; 0 errors = PASS (exit 0), errors or an early end exit 1. Live temperature, power and clock on a terminal. Ends by itself at the temperature limit (default 100 C), on a driver error, when the GPU stops answering or a piece of work takes more than 2 s; Ctrl+C stops it. Never changes clocks, voltages or fans, and never burns a software renderer (llvmpipe) as a GPU. Default 60 s. Asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR; before that only Vulkan's device list is read. \fB\-\-list\fR lists each back end's devices (starts the runtimes, runs no work).
.TP
\fBgpu\-compare\fR [\fB\-\-backends\fR \fIvulkan,hip,cuda,opencl\fR] [\fB\-\-seconds\fR \fIS\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
GPU Compute comparison: the same tests (matrix multiply FP32 / FP16 / FP64 with SRS's own kernel and the vendor library, multiply\-add, copy, upload, download, launch round trip) through every installed back end on the same GPU, side by side; fastest marked, percent of Vulkan; "CUDA (ZLUDA)" and one column per OpenCL driver. CUDA uses SRS's built-in kernels (PTX), so the NVIDIA runtime compiler (NVRTC) is not needed; an OpenCL driver without FP64 shows its FP64 rows as "not available". Asks first unless \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.TP
\fBcuda\-toolkit\fR [\fB\-\-check\fR|\fB\-\-install\fR|\fB\-\-ask\-again\fR|\fB\-\-never\-ask\fR]
The optional NVIDIA CUDA toolkit (NVIDIA's developer tools; SRS can use its runtime compiler, NVRTC, as a fallback): what it is for, whether NVRTC and a CUDA library (NVIDIA's or ZLUDA) are installed, and the install command for this distribution (Arch: pacman \-S \-\-needed cuda; Debian/Ubuntu: apt\-get install libnvrtc12 or libnvrtc11.2; Fedora, openSUSE: instructions). \fB\-\-install\fR checks first (package database lock, network, sudo/pkexec), needs a terminal and runs the command with sudo only after a typed \fByes\fR (twice with ZLUDA, where installing it is not recommended). \fB\-\-never\-ask\fR / \fB\-\-ask\-again\fR: whether the GUI asks about it (and gpu\-burn / gpu\-compare print a note). Nothing is ever installed without \fB\-\-install\fR.
.PP
\fBburn\fR, \fBbench\fR, \fBipi\fR, \fBlatency\fR, \fBpi\fR, \fBcrypto\fR, \fBprime\fR, \fBcachemem\fR, \fBstorage\fR, \fBos\-latency\fR, \fBcompute\fR, \fBwork\fR, \fBnet\fR, \fBgpu\fR, \fBgpu\-burn\fR and \fBgpu\-compare\fR load the CPU, the drive, the network or the GPU and only run when asked. All of them except \fBburn\fR (the burn\-in itself) also ask before they start, on stderr (\fBStart now? [y/N]\fR), unless \fB\-\-yes\fR is given; without a terminal and without \fB\-\-yes\fR they exit with 2 and start nothing. \fB\-\-help\fR after a command prints its usage; an unknown option exits with 2 without starting.
Run
.B sysreport-search \-\-help
for the full option list.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SRS_DOC_DIR
directory holding SRS-User-Guide.pdf and html/ (default /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/srs-sysinfo/
the user guide (SRS-User-Guide.pdf, html/SRS-User-Guide.html), CLI-REFERENCE.md and GLOSSARY.md.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR srs-gui (1),
.BR srs-sysreport (1)
